// Color Grading — 3-way Shadows/Midtones/Highlights color wheels plus a uniform Global wheel
// (Lightroom's successor to Split Toning). The 8th custom Core Image
// kernel in this codebase.
//
// Unlike SelectiveColor.cikernel (which selects by each pixel's own HUE),
// this kernel selects by each pixel's own LUMINANCE — a raised-cosine
// falloff over distance from 3 fixed anchors (0.0/0.5/1.0) on the
// PERCEPTUAL (sRGB-gamma-encoded) luma this stage actually operates on,
// since it runs inside applyPerceptualAdjustments's sandwich — NOT linear
// light. The falloff itself is the same mathematical FORM as
// SelectiveColor's `bandWeights` (which uses raised cosine over CIRCULAR
// hue distance) applied to a different axis and domain.
//
// Each of the first 4 arguments (Shadows, Midtones, Highlights, Global) is a vec4 for ONE tonal range: .rgb is that
// range's tint delta (already converted from the wheel's Hue/Saturation by
// ColorGradingTint.rgbDelta, Swift-side, ONCE per render — not per pixel),
// .w is that range's Luminance slider value. This kernel therefore needs
// NO rgb2hsl/hsl2rgb at all, unlike SelectiveColor — the wheel's own
// hue/saturation never needs reconstructing per pixel, only the WEIGHT
// each range gets at this pixel's own luminance.
//
// See EditPipeline.applyColorGrading for the Swift-side wrapper and the
// load-bearing pipeline-placement reasoning (must run AFTER Black & White's
// forced desaturation, so a B&W split-tone survives), and
// ColorGradingTint.swift for the Hue/Saturation -> RGB delta conversion.

float rangeWeight(float L, float center, float width) {
    float d = abs(L - center);
    if (d >= width) { return 0.0; }
    return 0.5 * (1.0 + cos(3.14159265 * d / width));
}

// `width` and `shift` are Lightroom's Blending and Balance, already mapped Swift-side by ColorGradingZones.
// - `width` is the raised-cosine falloff half-width (0.4 = the original fixed look; larger = the ranges overlap more).
// - `shift` MOVES THE MIDPOINT of the tonal scale while pinning both ends: luminance L in [0, 0.5] is stretched or
//   compressed onto [0, 0.5 + shift] and L in [0.5, 1] onto [0.5 + shift, 1], so true black stays fully Shadows and true
//   white fully Highlights whatever the Balance (a plain `L + shift` would instead strip the far wheel off the opposite end:
//   measured by the reviewer, white at Balance -1 kept only 0.309 of its Highlights weight). Positive shift = the scale looks
//   brighter, so the Highlights range reaches further down. shift == 0 skips the remap entirely.
// - When the three weights add up to more than 1 (only at widths above 0.5, where neighbouring ranges overlap enough to
//   double-count) the tint and luminance are divided by that sum, so Blending spreads the grade rather than making it
//   stronger. At the original width the sum never exceeds exactly 1.0 (at an anchor), so the division is by exactly 1.0
//   (the render still differs from the pre-Blending kernel by 1-2 ulp, see the comment at the division).
// The style tint pass (applyStyleTint) always passes the defaults (width 0.4, shift 0.0).
kernel vec4 colorGrading(__sample s, vec4 shadowTint, vec4 midtoneTint, vec4 highlightTint, vec4 globalTint, float width, float shift) {
    // The tint and luminance strengths stay fixed v1 defaults. MEASURED via a temporary spike (see
    // EditPipeline.applyColorGrading's doc comment for the real numbers and why each value was chosen), not guessed.
    float tintStrength = 0.8;
    float lumStrength = 0.5;

    float a = s.a;
    vec3 c = (a > 0.0) ? s.rgb / a : s.rgb;

    // Rec.709 luma on the RAW (unnormalized) pixel, clamped to [0,1] since
    // this is purely a WEIGHTING signal, not the pixel's own stored value.
    // Reviewer-caught: an earlier draft normalized `c` by its own max
    // channel before this dot product, copying SelectiveColor.cikernel's
    // headroom technique — wrong here, since a bright SATURATED pixel
    // (e.g. c=(1.5,0.1,0.1)) normalizes to (1.0,0.067,0.067), whose luma
    // (0.26) reads as "shadow," while the pixel's own real luma (0.398,
    // clamped) reads as "mid" — the correct classification. Unlike
    // SelectiveColor's HSL saturation formula, this dot product is
    // perfectly well-defined above 1.0 and needs no normalize at all.
    float L = clamp(dot(c, vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722)), 0.0, 1.0);
    // Balance: move the midpoint, keep both ends (see the header). Skipped at shift == 0 so the remap adds no arithmetic at the default (the render still drifts 1-2 ulp from the normalisation below).
    if (shift != 0.0) {
        L = (L < 0.5) ? L * (1.0 + 2.0 * shift) : (0.5 + shift) + (L - 0.5) * (1.0 - 2.0 * shift);
    }

    float wShadow = rangeWeight(L, 0.0, width);
    float wMid = rangeWeight(L, 0.5, width);
    float wHighlight = rangeWeight(L, 1.0, width);

    // Weighted sum of the three ranges' tints. It is unnormalized at widths up to 0.5 (the weights then never add up to
    // more than 1: the same reasoning SelectiveColor's own unnormalized sum documents — all-zero deltas give an exact
    // (0,0,0)/0 whatever the weights; in PRACTICE EditPipeline.colorGradingActive(_:) skips this kernel entirely once every
    // delta is zero, so that is defensive kernel-level correctness, not the mechanism behind the real identity guarantee,
    // which is Swift-side). Above 0.5 the sum is normalised below.
    // Divided by the weight sum only when it exceeds 1 (wide Blending): a division by exactly 1.0 at the original width. NOT
    // bit-for-bit free: the extra division changes how the compiler fuses the arithmetic, so a default render differs from the
    // pre-Blending kernel by 1-2 ulp (reviewer-measured on a 12.6M-float sweep: 324,643 floats differ, max 2.4e-7, i.e. far
    // below 8-bit quantisation; every other layout tried that kept the normalisation drifted as much or more).
    float norm = max(wShadow + wMid + wHighlight, 1.0);
    // The Global wheel (`globalTint`, same layout: rgb = tint delta, w = luminance) has weight exactly 1 at every pixel and is
    // added AFTER the normalised three-range sum, so Blending/Balance never reshape it. A zero vector adds exactly 0 in the SOURCE, but the compiled render still drifts 1-2 ulp from the pre-Global kernel (reviewer-measured on a 1024x1024 random image: 93,908 of 4,194,304 floats differ, max 2.4e-7), including the Style tint pass; a state with no wheel set skips this kernel entirely and is bit-identical.
    vec3 tint = (wShadow * shadowTint.rgb + wMid * midtoneTint.rgb + wHighlight * highlightTint.rgb) / norm + globalTint.rgb;
    float lum = (wShadow * shadowTint.w + wMid * midtoneTint.w + wHighlight * highlightTint.w) / norm + globalTint.w;

    // Added directly to the RAW pixel, NOT to a headroom-normalized-then-
    // rescaled value. Reviewer-caught: an earlier draft normalized by
    // `scale = max(max(c.r,c.g,c.b), 1.0)` before adding the delta and
    // rescaled by the same `scale` after — correct for SelectiveColor's
    // RELATIVE operations (hue rotation, saturation multiply, all inside
    // the normalized domain) but wrong for this kernel's ABSOLUTE additive
    // deltas: `(c/scale + delta) * scale == c + delta*scale`, silently
    // amplifying the tint/luminance strength by the pixel's own headroom
    // (measured: the same highlight tint read +0.4 perceptual on a 1.0
    // white but +0.4*1.1588 on a 1.4-linear white, an exposure-dependent
    // grade strength with no such dependency intended). Lower-bound clamp
    // only, preserving highlight headroom exactly like every other stage
    // in this pipeline's perceptual sandwich — the ADDED delta itself is
    // headroom-INDEPENDENT, unlike the old (wrong) construction.
    vec3 outRGB = max(c + tint * tintStrength + vec3(lum * lumStrength, lum * lumStrength, lum * lumStrength), vec3(0.0));
    return vec4(outRGB * a, a);
}
